%h1#title Support - Frequently Asked Questions
%h2 About Growstuff
%h3 Who runs Growstuff?
%p
  Growstuff was started by Alex Bayley (<a href="/members/skud">Skud</a>) an open source software developer and keen
  veggie gardener from Melbourne, Australia. It is now maintained by the community.
%h3 How did Growstuff get started?
%p
  The idea of Growstuff came out of a discussion at an open source conference in Spain in 2012. We wanted a free, open
  source of information about planting and growing crops, and realised the best way to do this would be to crowdsource
  it from gardeners around the world.
%p We first set up Growstuff as an open source project in August 2012, and quietly launched it in March 2013.
%h3 Is Growstuff a non-profit?
%p
  Growstuff is a community run project. You should also know that software and aggregated data about crops and who's
  growing them are freely available under open licenses.
%h3 Can I advertise on Growstuff?
%p Sorry, we're 100% ad-free!
%p
  If you're a member, you can post things to trade or sell in our trading post area, but we don't have and will never
  have banner ads or the like. We believe that supporting a website through outside advertising makes for a less
  pleasant experience for our members and visitors, and is not a sustainable business model.
%h3 What's the status of Growstuff? Is it in beta?
%p
  We don't much like the word "beta" or the idea it represents. Growstuff is in a constant state of change and
  improvement, and we hope we will always be adding new features and making things better for our members.
%p
  If you find any bugs or problems, please let us know via our support forum. If a feature you want isn't available yet,
  check back soon!
%h2 Membership and payments
%h3 Can I use Growstuff for free?
%p Absolutely! If you enjoy the site, we'd appreciate you considering a donation (see the link in the footer).
%h3 Can I get a membership for my family, school, or community group?
%p Yup! Feel free to sign up as "JonesFamily" or "SmithfieldCommunity" or whatever floats your boat.
%p
  Just keep in mind that you are responsible for anything that happens under that account, so be careful who you allow
  to use it. In due course, we are hoping to develop features to help community gardens share with their
  members/gardeners, so that they can track what's planted in the community garden without having to login as the
  community garden account. We'll publicise this widely when it's ready, so keep an ear to the ground.
%h2 Profile, settings, and privacy
%h3 How do I add a picture to my profile?
%p
  Profile pictures are provided by <a href="https://gravatar.com">Gravatar</a>, a service that provides "Globally
  Recognised Avatars", run by the people who operate the Wordpress blogging platform.
%p
  If you have ever signed up for Gravatar in the past, using the same address as you signed up for Growstuff, you will
  already have a picture for your profile.
%p
  If you have signed up for Growstuff with a different email address than your Gravatar address, you will need to add it
  on Gravatar.
%p
  If you have never signed up for Gravatar before, you can create an account and upload a picture, which will appear not
  only on Growstuff, but on any other site which supports Gravatars and where you use the same email address.
%h3 Do I have to fill in all this personal stuff?
%p
  Your profile asks several questions about you, which you can choose to answer (or not) as you see fit. We don't
  require personal information like your legal name, gender, or age for tracking or demographic purposes -- one of the
  benefits of not having ads on our site!
%p
  When it comes to location, it really helps if you can provide that to Growstuff because we use it to suggest things to
  grow. However, you can do it at whatever level of detail you're comfortable with: perhaps the name of your town, or
  your state or province, or even your country if the country is small enough and has a fairly consistent climate. Just
  choose something that is close enough to give you accurate growing advice, and vague enough to feel safe.
%p
  You can read more about the privacy of your information in our <a href="http://growstuff.org/policy/privacy">Privacy
  Policy</a>.
%h2 Crops, gardens, and planting
%h3 How can I add a new crop?
%p
  For now, please drop a note in <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/posts/skud-20130319-requests-for-new-crops">Requests
  for new crops</a> on our <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback &amp; Support
  forum</a>.
%h3 Will you add non-edible plants?
%p
  We do not currently have plans to add plants which are not edible or which are not "useful" in a food-growing or
  sustainability context -- these might include companion plants, green manure crops, plants used to manage pests or
  diseases, and the like. If you're thinking of purely ornamental plants, we probably won't be adding them anytime soon.
%p
  However, we are hoping, in future, to support the ability for any member to plant "unofficial" plants that aren't in
  our crop database.
%h2 Posting, commenting, and forums
%h3 What formatting can I use in posts and comments?
%p
  We use <a href="http://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/syntax/">Markdown</a> syntax, which offers a pretty wide
  range of options from simple emphasis (bold and italics) to links, pictures, and more. We're hoping to install a
  toolbar to help you generate the right syntax, but in the meantime I'm afraid all we can offer is the rather technical
  documentation linked above.
%h2 Forthcoming features
%h3 Where can I see what features are planned?
%p
  Our software developers use a tracking tool called Pivotal Tracker, and you can see ours at
  <a href="http://tracker.growstuff.org">http://tracker.growstuff.org/</a>. This tells you what features we are
  currently working on and plan to work on soon.
%p Pivotal Tracker's interface shows three columns of information:
%ul
  %li the "Current" section shows the features (called "stories") that we're working on at the moment
  %li "Backlog" shows you stories that we're planning to work on in the nearish future
  %li
    "Icebox" contains stories that we've identified as things we'd like to do, but which don't have a timeframe set yet.
%h3 Where can I suggest a feature I'd like to see?
%p
  We suggest checking the <a href="http://tracker.growstuff.org/">tracker</a> first (see above) to see if it's something
  we're already planning or talking about. Once you've done that, if it's not already planned or you have some other
  comments to make, you can post your suggestion in the <a href="/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback &amp;
  Support forum</a>.
%h2 Developers and API
%h3 Is there a Growstuff API?
%p
  At present there is no official API, as our site is still changing so rapidly. However, we do expose some of our data
  via JSON and/or RSS feeds, and you're welcome to play with it, on the understanding that it may change under you. You
  can find <a href="https://github.com/Growstuff/growstuff/wiki/API">API docs on our wiki</a>.
%p Use of our API and data is subject to the <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/policy/api">API and Data Use Policy</a>.
%h3 What license is your data under?
%p
  Growstuff's data is licensed under the <a href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">Creative Commons
  Attribution-ShareAlike (CC-BY-SA) 3.0 Unported license</a>. This means you can re-use it as long as you
  attribute it to Growstuff (by linking back to our site), and as long as your own app/site/project shares its own
  content or data under CC-BY-SA.
%p See our <a href="http://growstuff.org/policy/api">API and Data Use Policy</a> for more information.
%h2 Volunteering and jobs
%h3 Are you hiring?
%p Sadly, no. We're a self-funded/bootstrapped community group.
%p If you want to get involved, see below.
%h3 How can I volunteer to help Growstuff?
%p
  First and foremost, by being an active member of the site -- growing crops, tracking your harvests, posting in our
  forums, getting to know other members -- you help build our database and our community, without which Growstuff would
  be completely pointless. So, thanks for that!
%p
  Secondly, we'd love it if you could spread the word about Growstuff to your friends who grow their own food or who
  might like to try. You can see some tips on how to do that below.
%p
  Growstuff's software is built with the help of volunteer developers, too. If you're a coder or would like to learn how
  to code, you can join us in that. All are welcome, regardless of experience.
%p
  We also have volunteer crop wranglers, who manage our crops database, and moderators, who help keep the forums running
  smoothly. These are usually chosen from among our most active and helpful members.
%h3 How can I get involved as a coder?
%p
  Start by checking out our <a href="https://github.com/Growstuff/growstuff/wiki/">wiki</a>, code on
  <a href="http://github.com/Growstuff/growstuff">GitHub</a>, and <a href="http://tracker.growstuff.org">tracker</a> to
  get an idea of how we work.
%p
  If you want to get involved, you should join our
  <a href="http://lists.growstuff.org/mailman/listinfo/discuss">discussion mailing list</a>.
%h3 My coding skills are rusty or non-existent. Can I still get involved?
%p
  Sure! We love to mentor new coders, people who haven't coded in a while, or people who are just learning our platform
  (Ruby on Rails) for the first time. We work by pair programming, and can get you started on easy stories, starting
  with something as simple as fixing a typo or HTML tag (check the "easy" tag in our
  <a href="http://tracker.growstuff.org">tracker</a> for some examples).
%h3 How can I help spread the word about Growstuff?
%p
  First and foremost, by telling your friends and inviting them to join and be part of it. We don't want to be spammy
  about it, so we'll never ask to scrape your address book or automatically post Facebook updates or tweet on your
  behalf. But we would really love it if you'd tell people in your own words.
%p
  Places to spread the word online: Facebook, Twitter, and other social media; your own blog or website; gardening or
  sustainability forums you participate in.
%p
  Or offline: tell friends, family and neighbours; spread the word through your local community gardens, farmers
  markets, or produce swaps; contact your local sustainability or Transition group; or any other place where you think
  food-growers might be.
%h2 Terms of service, copyright, and other abuse
%h3 Where can I report a violation of the Terms of Service or Community Guidelines?
%p
  You can post in our <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback &amp; Support
  forum</a> or send email to <a href="mailto:support@growstuff.org">support@growstuff.org</a>.
%h3 What do you consider spam, and how can I report it?
%p
  There are many kinds of spam. The most obvious is repeated, unwanted commercial advertising. Many spammers also post
  irrelevant, nonsensical, or low-value content to forums to try and get you to click on a link, or just to see what
  they can get away with.
%p Please report all these kinds of spam!
%p
  For now, because we're so new, we don't have a good spam reporting mechanism set up. However, you can drop a link in
  our <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback &amp; Support forum</a> and we'll do
  what we can.
%h3 Someone posted something I believe is copyright. Where can I report it?
%p
  If you are the copyright owner, you can submit a complaint by following the procedure outlined in our
  <a href="http://growstuff.org/policy/copyright">Copyright Infringement Policy</a>.
%h3 Is Growstuff safe for kids to use?
%p
  As a website that's focused on growing food, we expect Growstuff to be a pretty safe and friendly environment.
  However, some parts of the site (such as posts made by members) may contain mature concepts, coarse language, and the
  like. If you don't want your kids exposed to these, we strongly suggest you supervise their use of Growstuff. We also
  suggest you be careful about what personal information your kids share on Growstuff.
%h2 Further support
%h3 Where can I find a list of known issues with the site?
%p In our Github <a href="https://github.com/Growstuff/growstuff/issues">Known Issues</a>.
%h3 My question's not answered here. Where can I ask for further support?
%p
  On our <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback &amp; Support forum</a>. Please
  check the list of known issues before posting.
%h3 Where can I post feedback/kudos?
%p
  We love feedback! Drop us a note in our <a href="http://www.growstuff.org/forums/growstuff-feedback-support">Feedback
  &amp; Support forum</a>. (Is there an echo in here?)
%h3 How else can I contact you?
%p
  If you need help with something that hasn't been covered here, then you can send an email to
  <a href="mailto:support@growstuff.org">support@growstuff.org</a>.
%p Media/press enquiries can go to <a href="mailto:media@growstuff.org">media@growstuff.org</a>.
%p
  If you have an enquiry about the site in general (eg. businessy stuff, or just want to get in touch with someone
  official in a non-support-like way), you can send an email to
  <a href="mailto:info@growstuff.org">info@growstuff.org</a>.
